illiams arXiv:2609.27583.\n\nenumeration and generationalgorithms and optimization\n\n#### References\n\n  1. F. Chung, P. Diaconis, and R. Graham. “Universal cycles for combinatorial structures.” Discrete Mathematics 110(1-3), pp. 43–59 (1992), DOI:10.1016/0012-365x(92)90699-g.\n  2. M. Engen and V. Vatter. “Containing All Permutations.” The American Mathematical Monthly 128(1), pp. 4–24 (2021), DOI:10.1080/00029890.2021.1835384, arXiv:1810.08252.\n  3. B. W. Jackson. “Universal cycles of k-subsets and k-permutations.” Discrete Mathematics 117(1-3), pp. 141–150 (1993), DOI:10.1016/0012-365x(93)90330-v.\n  4. B. Jackson, B. Stevens, and G. Hurlbert. “Research problems on Gray codes and universal cycles.” Discrete Mathematics 309(17), pp. 5341–5348 (2009), DOI:10.1016/j.disc.2009.04.002.\n  5. N. G. de Bruijn, A combinatorial problem, Proc. Kon. Ned. Akad. Wet. 49:7 (1946), 758–764.\n  6. D. Fillmore, B. Goeckner, R. Kirsch, K. Martin, and D. McGinnis. “The existence and structure of universal partial cycles.” Designs, Codes and Cryptography 94(7), Article 152 (2026), DOI:10.1007/s10623-026-01841-x.\n  7. T. Gerlach, L. Hartung, P. Herkenrath, J. Sawada, and A. Williams. “Shorthand Universal Tori for Permutations: Existence, Symmetry, and Generation of Twori.” arXiv preprint (2026), arXiv:2609.27583.\n\nFriday, November 13·15:30·0004·Chaired by Matthew Kwan\n\nStar Starred\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-24 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-10-24)\nciteturn11search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; 2026-10-24 v2 ... Finally, we prove that the zig-zag Eulerian polynomials, which enumerate swaps in alternating permutations, have simple negative roots, settling the real-rootedness conjecture of T. ... alternating permutation descent polynomial derangement derangement part eulerian polynomial gamma-polynomial interlacing real-rooted 