-----------------------------------\nau:Kallat_A in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AKallat_A+in%3Amath)\nciteturn11search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * A p-adic Valuation Calculus for Permutations with Restricted Cycle Lengths ... Oct 30 2026 math.CO math.NT arXiv:2610.40342v1 ... Mobius inversion writes the exponential generating function as an Euler product, and at a prime p the factors with non-p-integral exponents, the blocks, determine a finite knapsack problem. ...   * Universal Shift Congruences for Weighted Permutation Sequences ... The second conjecture asserts the shifted Gauss congruences $a(np^r+i)\\equiv a(np^{r-1}+i)\\pmod{p^r}$ for every $i\\ge0$, every prime $p$, and all $n,r\\ge1$.\n\n0\n\n@misc{2610.15849, author = {Ahaan Kallat}, title = {{U}niversal {S}hift {C}ongruences for {W}eighted {P}ermutation {S}equences}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2610.15849}, note = {arXiv:2610.15849v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe involution numbers $1,1,2,4,10,26,\\ldots$ repeat modulo $3$, $5$ and $7$. We explain this, and many similar patterns, by one rule. Many sequences count permutations in which every cycle of length $m$ carries a weight $w_m$ (involutions, derangements, \"sets of lists\", and many others in the OEIS). Writing $A(n)$ for such a count, the rule is $A(n+k)\\equiv A(n)A(k)$ modulo $\\mathrm{lcm}(n,k)$. It generalizes our earlier proof of the case modulo $k$ for one such sequence, but now it holds for all weights at once, as a coefficientwise congruence between universal polynomials, and we find the largest modulus that works for the universal polynomials, $nk/\\mathrm{rad}(\\gcd(n,k))$, and show that nothing larger does. We then show that passing from $k$ to $pk$ behaves like raising to the $p$-th power modulo a high power of $p$, with a sharp exponent for the polynomials, which gives $A(p^r)\\equiv(w_1+(p-1)w_p)^{p^{r-1}}$ modulo $p^r$ for odd $p$, and explains why the prime $2$ is special. -----------