25 prime knots with 11 to 13 crossings. One pinch move (a nonorientable band) to a slice knot or to a knot of four-genus one now settles 2,093 of them, 139 more than before; 1,432 stay open. The old verifier compared knot complements from one simplified triangulation and missed true pinches; forty simplifications under fixed seeds find a common triangulation. That change of rule is the run `knot-tables-pass3` of the modlog. The construction is `construct/pinch.py` with the 139 certificates; its refuter, the other smoothing of the same crossing, gives two components and fails.\n  * Bridge index, two walls. For the 293 open 13-crossing knots, colourings by Alexander quandles over F_p[t]/(g), p < 110, give at most three meridians, and none is the closure of a 3-braid in Murasugi normal form in the range searched. Both routes are recorded as failures.\n  * Release: conjecture-to-choose v1.1.0.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-09-23 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-09-23)\nciteturn11search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; 2026-09-23 ... In this paper, we study parking functions whose parking permutations avoid the patterns $1234$ and $2134$, obtaining an analog of the Schröder numbers for parking functions. ... This yields a complete proof of the conjecture proposed by Masuda--Rubio--Santiago \\cite{masuda2022permutation}.\n\nThis paper is devoted to studying permutation binomial $F_{r,a}(X)=X^r(X^{q-1}+a)\\in\\mathbb F_{q^e}[X]$ with $a\\in\\mathbb F_{q^e}^*$. We present a complete characterization for $F_{r,a}$ to be a permutation of $\\mathbb{F}_{q^e}$. This yields a complete proof of the conjecture proposed by Masuda--Rubio--Santiago \\cite{masuda2022permutation}. More precisely, we show that \\(F_{r,a}\\) permutes \\(\\mathbb F_{q^e}\\) if and only if $(-a)^{(q^e-1)/(q-1)}\\ne1, \\gcd(r,q-1)=1$, and there exists \\(1\\le h<e\\) with \\(\\gcd(h,e)=1\\) such that $r(q^h-1)\\equiv q-1\\pmod{q^e-1}.$\n\n2026-09-23\n\nGuillaume Ducoffe, Bogdan Dumitru\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nTowards a more structured search for Erdős-Gyárfás counter-examples\n\nThe Erdős-Gyárfás conjecture posits that every graph with minimum degree at least three contains a cycle of length some power of two. We prove a few simple structural properties for any minimal counter-example to this conjecture. In particular, the fraction of its vertices of degree three must be greater than $2/3$, thus improving on the prior bound of $4/7$ (Carr, 2026). Furthermore, it is either biconnected or the $1$-clique-sum of two biconnected graphs. By exploiting some of these properties, we were able to verify the conjecture for every graph of order at most $40$, every bipartite graph of order at most $66$, and every cubic graph of order at most $48$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper Watch — Mathematics, Physics and Biology | State of Proof (https://stateofproof.org/paper-watch?discipline=biology)\nciteturn11search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;     2026-09-01; added 2026-09-02 ...     a fresh full-resolution claim for a named number-theory conjecture, but without a public formalization, codebase, or certificate route on the primary record.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of unsolved problems in mathematics (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)\nciteturn11search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; * Superpermutations - smallest possible string of n digits that contains all possible permutations of n ... * Szymanski's conjecture: every permutation on the ${\\displaystyle n}$-dimensional doubly-directed hypercube graph can be routed with edge-disjoint paths. ... * The universality problem for C-free graphs: For which finite sets C of graphs does the class of C-free countable graphs 